PlotTrellis example data
========================

PlotTrellis's example project, also the example of the PlotTrellis paper: see
?pt_example. Five mouse genes, Eya2, Lrrfip1, Acot7, Dzip1 and Cxcl14, in two mouse
mammary epithelial populations, basal cells (Basal) and luminal
progenitors (LP), with every data type PlotTrellis draws: RNA-seq coverage, splice
junctions and transcript quantification, ATAC-seq coverage and peaks, H3K36me3 CUT&Tag
coverage and peaks with its IgG control, and Omni-C (Hi-C) contacts. mm39, GENCODE vM39.

Source
------
Milevskiy MJG, Coughlan HD, Kane SR, et al. Three-dimensional genome architecture
coordinates key regulators of lineage specification in mammary epithelial cells. Cell Genomics 2023;
3(11):100424. doi:10.1016/j.xgen.2023.100424. GEO SuperSeries GSE227750.

Every sample data file here is derived from that study's public reads (SRA, through GEO), processed for
PlotTrellis with the PlotTrellis-wf Nextflow workflow (GENCODE vM39 primary assembly, mm39),
biological replicates 1 and 2 of each assay:

    Assay               Basal                     LP
    RNA-seq             GSM7106766, GSM7106767    GSM7106769, GSM7106770
    ATAC-seq            GSM7142618, GSM7142619    GSM7142621, GSM7142622
    H3K36me3 CUT&Tag    GSM7142870, GSM7142871    GSM7142892, GSM7142893
    IgG CUT&Tag         GSM7142882, GSM7142883    GSM7142904, GSM7142905
    Omni-C (Hi-C)       GSM7142991, GSM7142992    GSM7142993, GSM7142994

The workflow writes one file per population and data type from both replicates (for
the transcript quantification, the mean of the two replicates' TPMs). Those files
were cut down to the five genes' windows below, values unchanged.

Windows (mm39; each gene plus half its length on each side, at least 25 kb, ends rounded
out to 10 kb):
    Eya2      chr2:165,340,001-165,710,000   (370 kb)
    Lrrfip1   chr1:90,860,001-91,130,000     (270 kb)
    Acot7     chr4:152,210,001-152,410,000   (200 kb)
    Dzip1     chr14:119,080,001-119,190,000  (110 kb)
    Cxcl14    chr13:56,410,001-56,470,000    (60 kb)
The GTF keeps the 47 genes overlapping these windows (349 transcripts). Only the five
genes above have all their data: a neighbouring gene that reaches past a window, a view
zoomed out past it, or a region elsewhere shows little or no data there (Hi-C reaches a
little further, see below). Where a window holds no
peak or junction line on its chromosome, the nearest line outside it is kept, so the
file still names that chromosome.

Files
-----
2.9 MB in all; the two .mcool files are 0.47 MB each.

samples.txt
    The project description PlotTrellis reads (hand-written). Paths in it are relative to
    this folder. Same columns as the workflow's own samples.txt, so code written against
    this example runs on the full data.

rna/{Basal,LP}_RNA.bw
    RNA-seq coverage, the bins overlapping the windows.

rna/{Basal,LP}_junctions.bed
    Splice junctions (BED12, read count in the score column), the lines overlapping the
    windows.

rna/{Basal,LP}_salmon/quant.sf
    Salmon transcript quantification (Name, TPM: the mean of the two replicates), only the
    rows of the transcripts in the GTF below. TPM is not renormalised, so it does not sum
    to one million here; every gene's transcript expression is as in the full file.

atac/{Basal,LP}_ATAC.bw, atac/{Basal,LP}_ATAC_peaks.narrowPeak
    ATAC-seq coverage (bins overlapping the windows) and peak calls (lines overlapping
    them).

cuttag/{Basal,LP}_H3K36me3.bw, cuttag/{Basal,LP}_IgG.bw,
cuttag/{Basal,LP}_H3K36me3_peaks.broadPeak
    H3K36me3 and IgG CUT&Tag coverage and H3K36me3 broad peaks, cropped the same way.

hic/{Basal,LP}_HiC.mcool
    Omni-C contacts with both ends in a wider window per gene (the gene's default view
    plus its width on each side, what a Hi-C track up to as tall as it is wide reads
    there), at the workflow's .hic bin sizes from 5 kb to 2.5 Mb (5, 10, 25, 50, 100,
    250 and 500 kb, 1 and 2.5 Mb; its 1 kb bins are left out, so Auto draws these genes
    at 5 kb), in the .mcool layout that PlotTrellis and plotgardener read (not checked
    with other cooler tools): raw counts, plus the .hic's KR, SCALE, VC and VC_SQRT normalisation vectors
    as bins columns, so each normalisation gives the .hic's values bin for bin. A view
    whose ends are not on bin edges can differ slightly at its edges, and so in its
    colour scale's top (Cxcl14's default view: 4.45 here, 4.46 from the .hic): the
    .mcool reader keeps the part-covered edge bins, the .hic reader leaves them out.
    One zero-count contact on the last bin of the file (the end of chr14) is added so
    the reader can read up to the last window.

annotation.gtf.gz
    Every GTF line of the genes overlapping the windows, plus the file header, from
    GENCODE mouse release M39, gencode.vM39.primary_assembly.annotation.gtf.gz.

annotation_chrom_sizes.rds
    mm39 chromosome lengths from UCSC (GenomeInfoDb::Seqinfo(genome = "mm39")), shipped so
    the first launch doesn't need to download them.

Licence and terms
-----------------
- GEO and SRA data: NCBI places no restrictions on the use or distribution of GEO data,
  though submitters may claim patent, copyright or other rights in the data they
  submitted (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/info/disclaimer.html). Please cite the
  paper above.
- GENCODE annotation and UCSC chromosome sizes: freely available without restriction.
- The processing and cropping are distributed with PlotTrellis under its licence
  (Artistic-2.0).

How it was made
---------------
data-raw/build-example-data.R in the PlotTrellis source repository rebuilds every
file here except samples.txt and this README, from the workflow's results folder, and
checks the bigWig and Hi-C crops against it.
